<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Simone Vignoso</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">14th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Simone Vignoso was a Genoese admiral who captured the island of Chios from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> in 1346 with a privately financed fleet. Because the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> could not repay the investors, the island was handed to them as a company, the Maona of Chios, which ruled it until the Ottoman conquest of 1566.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Vignoso is first mentioned in 1345 as admiral of a Genoese fleet raised against the Grimaldi, rebel Genoese nobles who had taken refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monaco">Monaco</a>. When the revolt failed and the nobles left Monaco for Marseille, the fleet was sent east to defend Genoese trade on the routes to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, and possibly to relieve the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Caffa</a>. The government had promised to reimburse the private citizens who owned and equipped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a>, and until then the fleet's loot and profits were to go to the owners.</p><p>Vignoso and his 29 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> in May and reached the Venetian colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Negroponte</a> on 8 June 1346. There they met the fleet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humbert-ii-of-viennois">Humbert II of Viennois</a>, part of the Second Smyrniote Crusade, who meant to take Chios, held by the Genoese Zaccaria family from 1304 to 1329, as a base for his anti-Turkish operations. The Genoese refused his offers of reward, attacked the crusader fleet, seized horses and equipment from it, and set out for Chios themselves.</p><p>They landed on 15 June and took the island within a week, except the citadel of the capital, which held out until 12 September. The treaty that came with its surrender left the Greek archons their estates, Byzantine privileges and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> churches in return for an oath to the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>. Within another week Vignoso took Old and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-phocaea">New Phocaea</a> on the Anatolian coast, rich from their alum mines, but his exhausted crews refused to go on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> and Tenedos.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>On 26 February 1347 Vignoso and the other stakeholders formed the Maona of Chios and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocaea">Phocaea</a>, a joint stock company to exploit the new possessions. A revolt of the Greek magnates in 1347 was put down, and their possessions were shared among the Genoese colonists and shareholders. Vignoso was the Maona's podestà in 1350, and nothing is known of him afterwards. The conquest gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> strategic bases in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, counterbalancing the long-established Venetian presence there, but it antagonised the Venetians and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> and was one of the main causes of the Third Genoese–Venetian War.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Simone Vignoso” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1346 a Genoese fleet under <b>Simone Vignoso</b>, financed by private shareholders, seized Chios from the Byzantines and also Old and New Phocaea on the Anatolian coast.</blockquote><a href="/state/maona-of-chios">Maona of Chios</a></li></ul></section>
